<article class="article-page">
<p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / Monitor arm sagging</p>
<p class="page-kicker">Monitor mounting</p>
<h1 class="page-title">Monitor arm sagging: check capacity and mounting before adding tension.</h1>
<p class="page-lead">A sagging display is not automatically a tension-screw problem. The arm may be outside its supported weight/depth range, the VESA plate may be loose, the desk attachment may be slipping, or the lift/tilt mechanism may simply need model-specific adjustment.</p>

<div class="article-summary"><strong>The five-minute answer</strong><p>Identify the exact arm model. Verify the monitor weight, depth, VESA pattern, and any attached accessories against that model’s specification. Inspect the VESA screws, arm joints, desk clamp/grommet, and desktop for movement or damage. If the load is supported and the mount is secure, follow the manufacturer manual to adjust the correct lift or tilt tension point.</p></div>

<div class="article-body">
<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 01</p><h2>Check the exact model’s load envelope.</h2>
<p>Do not apply a capacity from one arm to another. Even within Ergotron’s LX family, capacities vary by model, and Ergotron notes that monitor depth can reduce capacity on some versions. Match the model number to the current product sheet/manual.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 02</p><h2>Find where the movement actually occurs.</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>Screen tilts downward</strong><p>Inspect the tilt joint and VESA plate before adjusting tilt tension.</p></div>
<div><strong>Whole arm lowers</strong><p>Inspect the lift mechanism and verify the display is within its supported load range.</p></div>
<div><strong>Base moves on the desk</strong><p>Stop adjusting arm tension. Check the clamp/grommet, desktop material, thickness, and damage.</p></div>
</div></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 03</p><h2>Inspect VESA hardware and the desk attachment.</h2>
<p>Power down the display if you need to support or remove it. Check that the correct VESA screws/spacers are seated and that the monitor plate is fully engaged. Check the desk clamp or grommet for slip. If the desktop is cracking, crushing, bowing, or delaminating, remove the load and address the mounting surface before reuse.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Step 04</p><h2>Adjust only the documented tension point.</h2>
<p>Arm designs use different fasteners and directions. Follow the exact manual. Support the display while adjusting, make small changes, and test throughout the arm’s travel. Do not use maximum torque as a substitute for a capacity mismatch.</p></section>

<section><p class="page-kicker">Decision</p><h2>Do you need a new arm?</h2>
<div class="article-cards">
<div><strong>No</strong><p>If the supported arm holds position after correct tension and hardware adjustment, keep it.</p></div>
<div><strong>Maybe</strong><p>If the display is within weight but unusually deep, curved, or accessorized, check whether the manufacturer derates capacity for that geometry.</p></div>
<div><strong>Yes</strong><p>If the display falls outside the model’s supported load/mount range or the arm cannot hold a supported load after correct assembly, choose a properly rated arm or seek service.</p></div>
</div></section>

